WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.810
Wasps.

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.320
Locusts.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.819
Spiders.

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:06.330
Ants.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:13.100
These insects and the almost 900,000 others
like them, make up 80 percent of the world’s

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.100
species.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.970
Seen or unseen, the creepy crawlies, flying
insects, and burrowing creatures are necessary

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:23.439
to our ecosystems and can contribute positively
to our everyday lives.

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:28.829
But, they can be devastating menaces, spreading
disease, and causing famine.

00:00:28.829 --> 00:00:32.630
Despite my own misgivings, when our friends
at Deep Look invited me to get up close and

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:38.126
personal with these destructive insects—I
couldn’t let you all down and say no.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.900
There is perhaps no more infamous insect swarm
than the locust.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.260
Appearing in mass since at least the days
of Ancient Egypt, the migrating insects will

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.670
tear through crops in their hunger.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:04.830
Tales of destructive locusts plagues, e, go
back thousands of years, prominent in the

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.399
Bible and the Quran.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:10.140
Unsurprisingly, these real-life anxieties
have been the subjects of science fiction

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.340
and horror narratives for hundreds of years—taking
on monstrous form.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.890
In Edgar Allan Poe’s “The Gold-Bug”
the main character is driven to madness after

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.749
being bitten by a mysterious gold beetle.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:27.319
H.G. Wells' story “The Empire of the Ants,”
features an intelligent, aggressive Amazonian

00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:30.960
ant swarm—one that will purposefully attack
humans.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:37.420
But bugs became visibly monstrous on screen
in the 1950s—when the “big bug” subgenre

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.030
of science fiction and horror was hatched.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:45.909
But why did this decade in particular latch
on to the idea of freakishly monstrous bugs?

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:56.130
The 1950s monstrous insect Renaissance peaked
most notably with 1954’s Them!

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:01.560
While not the first of these bugs gone rogue
kinds of films, it was certainly the most

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.560
influential.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.999
Them! was a commercial and critical success
and was Warner Bros. highest grossing film

00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:10.640
of 1954.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.430
In the movie, the FBI, police, government
officials, and scientists discover a nest

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:26.319
of giant ants at a nuclear testing site.

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:40.190
They try to hide the discovery using cyanide
bombs--but two queens survive.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.209
They and their offspring are driven from the
desert to the city of Los Angeles, where they

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:46.660
are eventually killed with flamethrowers.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.689
By today’s standards the special effects
and even the plot seem underwhelming, but

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:55.610
at the time viewers and film critics found
the story believable.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:00.860
One reviewer from the New York Times described
the film as “tense, absorbing, and surprisingly

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.920
enough, somewhat convincing.”

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.019
Another from The Saturday Review wrote that
the film was “as persuasively realistic

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:10.590
a horror story as one could possibly imagine.”

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:15.290
Why so convincing and realistic? The nuclear
test site origins of the giant ants make this

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.909
movie a commentary on the impact of atomic
energy and nuclear weapons.

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:25.230
While Japanese kaiju films emerged following
WWII as a response to the horrors of the destruction

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.609
of nuclear bombs, the American-made Them!
is more about the unknown lingering effects

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:36.870
of nuclear testing—and arguably, the guilt
felt for developing and using nuclear weapons.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:42.060
Many of the big bug films that followed Them!
incorporated nuclear weapons or radiation.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:47.750
Like 1957’s Beginning of the End, which
features massive locusts who grew to a freakish

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:53.700
size after eating giant vegetables grown with
radioactive fertilizer—fertilizer produced

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.790
by the US government’s experimental farm.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.540
As the locusts continue to feed on the products
of other radioactive experiments, their size

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.760
continues to increase.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.950
After they’ve torn through all vegetable
life—they turn to humans.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.749
Moving from the countryside into Chicago they
destroy all in their path before finally being

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:15.150
lured to Lake Michigan and killed.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.530
The lingering effects of atomic energy and
nuclear weapons weren’t the only cultural

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:22.120
commentary present in these films.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:27.690
Between 1940 and 1970, as part of the Great
Migration, over 4 million Black Americans

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.610
moved from the South to cities in the Northeast
and West, like Chicago, New York, Detroit,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.139
and Los Angeles.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:39.190
The height of this migration­—the 1950s—was
marked by a period of what is commonly referred

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.380
to as “white flight,” the increased movement
of millions of White Americans away from inner

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.090
cities to newly constructed suburbs.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:51.460
These movies mirrored the anxieties of white
audiences who viewed the Great Migration as

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:57.020
a type of invasion, a theory supported by
the very real violence White urbanities inflicted

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:03.120
on minority residents and some science fiction
films seemed to support this racist viewpoint

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.170
Driven in part by federal policy and economic
processes, the socioeconomic divide between

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:13.180
White and non-White citizens was reinforced,
and broadened, by the physical movement of

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.280
both populations.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.620
This movement is mirrored in the on-screen
insects’ journey from a more rural landscape

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.180
to suburbs or cities.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.900
The postwar movement’s outlawing of racial
segregation also seems significant.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.169
The Brown v. Board of Education ruling was
in 1954—the same year Them! became a box

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:32.580
office success.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.110
These Big Bug movies have also been read as
a response to growing fears of Communism from

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:42.349
the Cold War, with the insects standing in
as a metaphor for perceived foreign predators

00:05:42.349 --> 00:05:48.780
secretly embedded in society, invading quietly
before rising in mass to destroy Americans.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.840
Some of these films suggest this more explicitly,
like in The Deadly Mantis (1957), where the

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.610
titular bug’s movements from the Arctic
Circle are detected on radar meant to monitor

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.940
the Russian military.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.190
These films also play on the fear of the crowd.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:09.220
The concept of the “crowd” only solidified
at the turn of the 20th century, when Industrialism

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:14.789
and urbanization contributed to the collective
anxiety of the masses, of being a cog in the

00:06:14.789 --> 00:06:16.160
machine.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.319
On-screen these masses became invading aliens
and swarms of insects.

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:24.819
In The Naked Jungle (1954), the ants aren’t
abnormally sized or mutated; there are just

00:06:24.819 --> 00:06:29.300
a lot of them—a massive “forty square
miles” sized swarm.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.840
The giant wasp swarm created from cosmic rays
in The Monster from Green Hell is another

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:34.840
example.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.410
While many big bug films featured singular
monstrous arthropods, like The Black Scorpion,

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:45.229
Tarantula!, and The Earth v. the Spider, those
creatures' real-life counterparts are poisonous

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:46.229
to humans.

00:06:46.229 --> 00:06:51.340
It is the smaller insects that swarm in these
films—it is the masses that are more difficult

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.639
to defeat.

00:06:52.639 --> 00:06:56.490
Another interpretation that focuses on the
arthropods at the heart of these movies is

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.539
the cultural conversation surrounding the
pesticide industry.

00:06:59.539 --> 00:07:05.080
In an era of literal movement (from rural
to city, or city to suburb) where the domestic

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:10.159
space was prioritized, people were freaked
out about regulating bug populations.

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:16.509
The 1950s marked a boom in pest control with
etymologists, agricultural associations, and

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:22.069
government organizations, not to mention chemical
executives, promoting the destructive potential

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:23.860
of insects.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.680
Even small, seemingly harmless populations
of bugs that live quietly below ground, largely

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:35.030
out of sight of humans, could become a physical
threat as their populations literally grew.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.419
Increased populations of locusts, Japanese
beetles, boll weevils, fire ants, and gypsy

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:43.760
moths were all targeted in the United States
during the 50s.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:49.599
The desire to eradicate these creatures was
even referred to as the “War on insects.”

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:55.780
When DDT was developed as a synthetic insecticide
in the 1940s, it was literally promoted as

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:58.400
“the atomic bomb of insecticides.”

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.610
But by the time the Big Bug films emerged,
it was already becoming apparent that not

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:07.690
only did these chemicals have a negative effect
on all life—they also weren’t as effective

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.370
as previously believed.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:14.810
Some of the Big Bug films even incorporated
this revelation, showing large dosing of insecticides

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:22.170
to have little effect on the creatures.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.849
To put it simply, bugs were everywhere and
we wanted them dead.

00:08:25.849 --> 00:08:29.930
The defeat of giant insects on the silver
screen was a welcome image.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:33.380
The role of the military in these films is
also interesting.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:38.180
While plot details often include government
cover ups or secret experiments, in the same

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:42.140
films the military ends up killing the bugs
and saving the day.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.490
Science is portrayed similarly.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:49.150
While a “mad scientist” is behind the
helm of the bug's mutations, other scientists

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:53.490
are responsible for coming up with the solutions
to eliminate them.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:58.780
1953’s Mesa of Lost Women features mutant
spider women and giant spiders created by

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:04.770
a scientist's pituitary experiments.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:13.200
Tarantula’s scientist possessed altruistic
motives—his experiments aimed at combating

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.650
world hunger and increasing lifespan.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:21.640
Likewise, Beginning of the End’s scientist
also wanted to solve world hunger—with radiation.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.110
The Big Bug films also presented audiences
with a basic fear —these were creatures

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:29.020
that they could encounter in their own backyards.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:34.220
While giant rampaging lizards, aliens, and
deep-sea creatures were scary, they also maintained

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:35.990
a greater sense of escapism.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.480
Insects, on the other hand, are a part of
daily life.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.269
While insects aren’t going to hunt down
humans to eat them alive, they do pose a threat.

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:48.950
Besides the obvious poisonous ones and their
harmful bites, swarms of insects can also

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:51.470
damage human lives and livelihoods.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:55.769
While honeybees, gnats, and termites are all
among the species that swarm, some of the

00:09:55.769 --> 00:09:59.730
most damaging (and notorious) are fire ants

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:05.060
Fire ants swarm as part of their reproductive
cycle, forming strange floating masses.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:09.940
Their powerful and painful bites make them
a threat to humans and animals, and they can

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:11.680
decimate crops.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:17.290
Originally from South America, following World
War I, fire ants traveled to the United States.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:22.370
But it wasn’t until, you guessed it, the
1950s when the invasion gained attention.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:28.120
In 1957 the federal government targeted fire
ants with their own bug-killing propaganda

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:29.120
films.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.399
So what about stories featuring our beloved
Locusts?

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:38.060
Locusts are actually a variety of grasshopper
species that undergo dramatic transformation

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:42.971
when environmental circumstances are just
right, locusts swarms can number into the

00:10:42.971 --> 00:10:43.971
billions.

00:10:43.971 --> 00:10:50.570
So I went to a lab to learn about locusts—annndddd
ended up holding one.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:53.959
I may or may not have screamed when one unexpectedly
jumped on me.

00:10:53.959 --> 00:10:58.760
I could feel its little claw tips.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:02.180
I hated it.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:07.260
After 1957’s Beginning of the End, locusts
interestingly faded away from science fiction

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.899
narratives—until the 21st century.

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:15.630
As global warming causes locust swarms to
increase, we have again found them on our

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:16.950
screen (Locusts: The 8th Plague; The Swarm).

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:20.580
But it's not just these monstrous flying creatures
that endure.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:26.980
Following their golden era in the 1950s, big
bug horror continuously plagues horror narratives.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:31.910
From The Mist to 8-Legged Freaks, The Swarm
to Arachnophobia—it doesn’t seem like

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.730
we will be squashing these monsters anytime
soon.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:39.750
But after all, it’s hard to dismiss something
that infiltrates our lives everyday—seen

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:43.120
or unseen, we live in a bug-eat-bug world.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.709
Thanks again to our friends over at Deep Look
for their really close up view of the locust.

00:11:47.709 --> 00:11:52.240
Most of the time, desert locusts are solitary,
mild-mannered insects.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.899
But under *just* the right conditions… they
have an explosive dark side.

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:01.899
Those conditions trigger a hormone that changes
their bodies - and behavior--creating plagues

00:12:01.899 --> 00:12:03.850
of biblical proportions.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:04.910
And guess what?

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.600
Your body makes that same hormone!

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.250
Check out this new episode from Deep Look
that gets up close and personal with these

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:53.869
incredible insects.

